In Confidence

Ms Nadia Zahmoul

Via Email

=20

19 November 2025

=20

Dear Ms Zahmoul

=20

Your complaint about HM Courts and Tribunals Service (HMCTS)

=20

Thank you for giving us information on your complaint about HMCTS.=20

=20

Our role

=20

We make final decisions on complaints that the NHS in England, UK governmen=
t departments and other UK public organisations have not resolved. Before we=
 look at a complaint, we usually expect that you have complained in full to =
the organisation and you have received a final response from it.

=20

We recognise that everyone has different needs and circumstances, and these=
 are likely to influence the way you access our service.  If you need this l=
etter in a different format or a reasonable adjustment in how we communicate=
 with you, please contact me to discuss your accessibility needs. You can us=
e the details listed above or our Sign Video service  to contact me.

=20

Our decision

=20

We have looked at everything you sent us and we consider your complaint is =
not yet ready for us. We need to give you some further advice about how to g=
et a response to your complaint from HMCTS before we can give it a more deta=
iled consideration.=20

=20

This is because it appears you have not yet completed HMCTS=E2=80=99s complaints =
process. =20

=20

It is important that HMCTS has the chance to look at your complaint, give y=
ou a response, and put right any mistakes. Often this is the quickest way to=
 resolve complaints. Therefore, your complaint is not yet ready for us and w=
e cannot take any further action at this time. =20

=20

I have checked over the information you have submitted. I can clearly see y=
ou have asked HMCTS questions. Relating to the handling and administration o=
f your court of appeal case. HMCTS have responded to you twice and on 30/10/=
2025 signposted you to the third and final stage in their complaints process=
. I have not yet got a copy of a response from the third stage. Therefore, t=
he case is not yet ready for us to investigate.=20

=20

Please note in order for us to investigate a HMCTS complaint we will also n=
eed the case to be referred to us via a sitting MP. I do not have a counters=
ignature from an MP on your complaint form, or any evidence a referral has b=
een accepted. This is a requirement under our legislation. Once you have the=
 response to the third tier of the complaint this should be sent to your loc=
al MP to complete the process.

=20

What you can do next

=20

To complete the complaints process, you should write to the HMCTS User Inve=
stigations Team and ask them to look at your concerns. Their address is:=20

HM Courts & Tribunals Service,=20

User Investigations Team,=20

6th Floor (6.12),=20

102 Petty France=20

London,=20

SW1H 9AJ

E: userinvestigations@justice.gov.uk

=20

Getting help to make your complaint

=20

If you have not already complained, you can find some tips on our website: =
  =20

www.ombudsman.org.uk/complain-for-change/our-resources/tips-on-making-a-com=
plaint-about-uk-government-services

=20

If you need help putting your complaint together, you can speak to the Citi=
zens Advice Bureau. You can find your local branch at www.citizensadvice.org=
.uk.

=20

What you can do after you get a response=20

=20

If you are still unhappy after you receive a response from HMCTS, you shoul=
d then ask your local MP to send the complaint to us. By law you must refer =
your complaint to an MP within one year of becoming aware of the issues you =
complain about. =20

=20

We can extend this time limit if we think it is reasonable to do this. The =
things we consider include your reasons for not complaining earlier and how =
long the local complaints process took. We will also look at why you did not=
 make the complaint to the MP within a year.=20

=20

Once we have all the information we need to progress your complaint, you wi=
ll then be informed of the next steps.=20

=20

For further information on how we deal with complaints once they are progre=
ssed, please visit our website https://www.ombudsman.org.uk/making-complaint=
/how-we-deal-complaints.

Please note there are some important details about how we use your informat=
ion at the bottom of this email.

=20

Yours sincerely

=20

Daniel Shelton

Intake Caseworker

Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman

T: 0345 015 4033

E: phso.enquiries@ombudsman.org.uk

W: www.ombudsman.org.uk

=20

Important details about how we use your information

=20

You can read more about how we use your information in our privacy notice. =
The notice explains how we use and look after information about you, or that=
 could identify you, and how long we keep it. It also explains your rights a=
nd how to request your information. You can find the privacy notice online a=
t www.ombudsman.org.uk/information-you-give-us. If you would like a copy in =
a printed or other format, please contact informationrights@ombudsman.org.uk=
 or call 0345 015 4033.

=20

You can find out more about how we deal with complaints in our detailed gui=
de on our website: Go to www.ombudsman.org.uk/making-complaint/how-we-deal-c=
omplaints. Further information about how we consider evidence in complaints =
can be found here: https://www.ombudsman.org.uk/publications/balancing-evide=
nce-guidance.

=20

Information on how we use clinical advice to inform our decision can be fou=
nd here: www.ombudsman.org.uk/how-we-use-clinical-advice-inform-our-decision=
s

If you would like me to send you a copy of these documents, please let me k=
now.=20

=20

Our Service Charter also describes what service you can expect from us. Go =
to www.ombudsman.org.uk/about-us/our-service-charter to find out more.

=20

=20


